WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.640
The lining of a mouse's small intestine is replaced every few days.

2
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.700
Yet the animal keeps that lining for life.

3
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.540
Cells are born in a pocket called a crypt.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.580
They move up and are shed at the tip.

5
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.700
Something at the bottom of each crypt never runs out.

6
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.160
Which cells are they, and how would you prove it?

7
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:30.250
Expressing a gene tells you nothing about fate.

8
00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.190
So scientists trace cells instead.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:39.510
A drug called tamoxifen switches on a permanent mark in a cell at the crypt base.

10
00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.750
Every daughter cell inherits that mark.

11
00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.450
Over 60 days, the label filled ribbons of epithelium.

12
00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.550
Those ribbons contained every kind of epithelial cell.

13
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.870
That shows multipotency within the gut.

14
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.370
It does not show pluripotency.

15
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.870
How do the stem cells share the crypt?

16
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:06.270
One cartoon says each stem cell divides unevenly, every time.

17
00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.750
One stem cell, and one short-lived cell.

18
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.950
In 2010, Hugo Snippert and colleagues tested it.

19
00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.570
Their reporter gives each clone a random colour, one of four.

20
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.510
These stem cells divide about once a day.

21
00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:29.550
Most divisions were symmetric, giving two stem cells or two non-stem cells.

22
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:37.843
Then they waited. Over one to six months, crypts drifted toward a single colour.

23
00:01:38.763 --> 00:01:41.603
Surviving clones grew, while others vanished.

24
00:01:42.663 --> 00:01:45.123
This pattern is called neutral drift.

25
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:49.403
Equivalent stem cells crowd one another out at random.

26
00:01:49.403 --> 00:01:54.323
Chance, not a fixed hierarchy, decides which lineage wins.

27
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:58.453
Now the arithmetic, with round numbers.

28
00:01:59.673 --> 00:02:03.733
Label one stem cell in each of 100 crypts.

29
00:02:03.973 --> 00:02:07.293
Say each crypt holds five equivalent stem cells.

30
00:02:08.153 --> 00:02:12.993
A labelled clone takes over its crypt with probability 1 in 5.

31
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:17.233
So expect 20 fully labelled crypts.

32
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:19.113
The other 80 clones are lost.

33
00:02:20.113 --> 00:02:23.493
Check the count. 100 labelled cells at the start.

34
00:02:23.493 --> 00:02:28.193
20 crypts times 5 cells makes 100 at the end.

35
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:35.163
Change 5 to 14, and the expected count drops to about 7 crypts.

36
00:02:35.523 --> 00:02:41.783
14 is roughly the number of Lgr5-high cells counted per crypt in the mouse small

37
00:02:41.783 --> 00:02:46.923
intestine. 5 was an effective number for the model, smaller than that count.

38
00:02:47.863 --> 00:02:52.603
So counting fully labelled crypts estimates how many cells really compete.

39
00:02:53.603 --> 00:02:56.723
Balance is kept by the population, not by each division.
